title: 'Decide: should an allow rule for a wrapper command cover the wrapped command?'
updated: 2026-11-23T02:48:50Z
---

Found while writing the unreagent-permissions skill (k199), confirmed by a probe test. Bash(sudo *) allows sudo rm -rf /. isWrapper is applied on the deny side only (deny sees through sudo/env/timeout), whereas a nested shell script does need its own allow cover. Bash(timeout *) or Bash(env *) are plausible rules a user writes without meaning to allow everything behind them.

Options:
1. Keep the behaviour and document it in the README Permission layer section: an allow rule for a wrapper allows whatever it wraps.
2. Require allow cover for the wrapped command as well, as for nested shells. Stricter, and it changes what existing allow rules grant.

Recommendation (main agent): 2 matches the fail-closed stance of the layer, but it is a behaviour change for existing configs, so it is the maintainer call.

Decision 2026-11-21 (maintainer, quoted): 'ok dann die strengere variante'. Option 2: an allow rule for a wrapper command (sudo, env, timeout and the rest of isWrapper) must not grant the wrapped command; the wrapped command needs its own allow cover, as a nested shell script does. Behaviour change for existing configs: needs a Changes entry and a README note. Owner: unreagent-worker, failing table rows first (skill unreagent-permissions). Same file as k127 (in review, rewritten splitter not on main) and k209: runs after k127 lands.
